1. Explain how $k$-fold cross-validation is implemented.
K-fold cross-validation is implemented by randomly dividing a set of 
observations into k groups of "folds". First, you hold out 1 fold, and then 
fit your model on remaining k - 1 folds. Second, you predict the held-out
fold, calculate k mean square error's for the left out fold for each fold. 

2. What are the advantages and disadvantages of $k$-fold cross-validation relative to
    
    a. The validation set approach?
  The validation estimate of the test error is highly variable, as it depends
  on which observations were heldout. Also, only a subset is used to fit
  the model. Since statistical models tend to do better with more data, the
  validation set approach can overestimate the test error, and both these 
  are addressed through the use of k-fold cross-validation. The validation
  set approach is advantaged over k-fold CV though when computational cost 
  is of utmost concern, such as with large datasets or complex models. This
  is because validation set approach only requires training the model once on 
  the chosen validation split, while k-fold requires training the model
  multiple times for each fold. 

    b. LOOCV?
  LOOCV is a special case of k-fold in which k=n. The advantage of k-fold is
  computational advantage, where we have to fit model k-times (not n). Also
  another advantage is due to the bias-variance trade off, where LOOCV has 
  higher variance than k-fold. An advantage of LOOCV over k-fold is that it 
  offers a more robust estimate of model performance with significantly less
  bias since almost the entire dataset is used (n-1 observations) for training
  in each fold. This is much better for smaller datasets where k-fold might
  use too few samples for training. 
    
