    d. Write a for loop from $i = 1$ to $i = n$ where $n$ is the number of observations in the data set that performs each of the following steps:
    
    
        i. Fit a logistic regression model that predicts `Direction` using `Lag1` and `Lag2` using the `Weekly` data set *using all but the $i$th observation*.
        
        ii. Predict the direction of the $ith$ observation.
        
        iii. Determine whether or not an error was made in predicting the direction for the $i$th observation. If an error was made, indicate this as a $1$ and if not, a $0$.
        
    e. Take the average of the $n$ numbers obtained in d. iii. to obtain the LOOCV estimate for the test error. Comment on the results.
    
```{r}
## i 

n_obs <- nrow(Weekly)

#initialize storage vector 
error_indicators <- numeric(n_obs)

#loop part
for(i in 1:n_obs) {
  m_loocv <- workflow() |>
    add_model(logistic_spec) |>
    add_recipe(logistic_recipe) |>
    fit(Weekly[-i, ])
   ## isolate and predict ith observation 
  test_observation <- Weekly[i, ]
  
  ## predicted class
  predicted_direction <- predict(m_loocv, new_data = test_observation, type = "class")
  ## true direction 
  true_direction <- Weekly[i, ]$Direction 
  #extract predicted direction from tibble output
  pred_class <- predicted_direction$.pred_class
  
  #determine error
  is_error <- ifelse(pred_class != true_direction, 1, 0)
  
  #store results
  error_indicators[i] <- is_error
  }
loocv_error_estimate <- mean(error_indicators)

cat("LOOCV Test Error Estimate:", loocv_error_estimate, "\n")

```
 .45 represents the average misclassification rate of this model when 
 predicting a single, unseen observation. This represents the proportion of 
 weeks in the dataset that the logistic regression model using Lag1 and Lag2
 incorrectly predicted the market direction. Since direction is a binary 
 variable, a random guess would yield an error rate of 50%. Since the LOOCV
 test error estimate is 45%, it provides little predictive power. This
 model is a highly reliable (low bias) estimate of the true test error since
 the model was trained on almost the entire dataset (n-1 obs) for every 
 prediction.
  
